WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.800
WebSockets.

2
00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:03.936
Server push is easy with SSE.

3
00:00:03.936 --> 00:00:08.367
But what happens when the browser needs to answer on the same channel?

4
00:00:09.567 --> 00:00:13.533
A WebSocket starts life as a normal HTTP request.

5
00:00:13.833 --> 00:00:15.400
The client asks to upgrade.

6
00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:18.167
The server agrees, on the same connection.

7
00:00:18.467 --> 00:00:22.767
Now it's a persistent channel, and either side can send a message at any time.

8
00:00:23.967 --> 00:00:25.600
Let's put them side by side.

9
00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:27.215
SSE pushes one way.

10
00:00:27.215 --> 00:00:30.467
A WebSocket carries traffic in both directions.

11
00:00:30.767 --> 00:00:32.386
SSE reconnects for free.

12
00:00:32.386 --> 00:00:36.300
With WebSockets, you write the reconnect and resync logic.

13
00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:42.033
And a WebSocket makes the server hold per connection state, like memory and file descriptors.

14
00:00:43.233 --> 00:00:44.800
So here's the decision rule.

15
00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:46.967
Ask: does the client talk back?

16
00:00:47.267 --> 00:00:49.577
If it's server push only, use SSE.

17
00:00:49.577 --> 00:00:52.500
If it's genuinely two way, use a WebSocket.

18
00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:55.167
Scaling gets harder too.

19
00:00:55.467 --> 00:01:00.267
Two users connected to different replicas can't see each other's messages directly.

20
00:01:00.567 --> 00:01:03.367
They need a shared pub sub backbone, like Redis.

21
00:01:04.567 --> 00:01:06.367
Here's a tiny broadcast server.

22
00:01:06.667 --> 00:01:09.633
We accept the connection and add the client to a set.

23
00:01:09.933 --> 00:01:14.767
Then we loop, receive a message from one client, and send it to every connected tab.

24
00:01:15.067 --> 00:01:17.667
When the connection drops, we remove the client.

25
00:01:18.867 --> 00:01:20.300
Here's how that plays out.

26
00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:25.238
Your telemetry dashboard began as SSE, with frames pushed one way.

27
00:01:25.238 --> 00:01:25.800
Perfect.

28
00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:32.567
Then operators wanted to acknowledge alerts and adjust thresholds, with every other operator seeing it instantly.

29
00:01:32.867 --> 00:01:34.686
That's two way, shared state.

30
00:01:34.686 --> 00:01:38.700
So you moved to a WebSocket, with Redis pub sub across replicas.

31
00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:40.693
Let's recap.

32
00:01:40.693 --> 00:01:43.667
A WebSocket is a persistent, two way channel.

33
00:01:43.967 --> 00:01:46.700
It costs connection state and resync logic.

34
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:49.500
Scaling needs pub sub across replicas.

35
00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:51.262
Push only, use SSE.

36
00:01:51.262 --> 00:01:53.954
Genuinely two way, use a WebSocket.

37
00:01:53.954 --> 00:01:55.800
Next up: authentication.
